  "solutions": [
    {
      "kind": "BRUTE_FORCE",
      "language": "JAVASCRIPT",
      "title": "Scan all accepted requests",
      "intuition": "Count the client's accepted requests in the window by looking at all of them.",
      "approach": "Keep a list of accepted [client, time]. For each request, count matching entries with time > t - window and compare with limit.",
      "pseudocode": "accepted = []\nfor (c, t): recent = count of (c, s) in accepted with s > t - window; if recent >= limit: out false else accepted.push; out true",
      "code": "function rateGate(limit, window, requests) {\n  const accepted = [];\n  return requests.map(([client, time]) => {\n    let recent = 0;\n    for (const [c, t] of accepted) if (c === client && t > time - window) recent++;\n    if (recent >= limit) return false;\n    accepted.push([client, time]);\n    return true;\n  });\n}",
      "timeComplexity": "O(n²)",
      "spaceComplexity": "O(n)",
      "commonMistakes": [
        "Counting rejected requests, which keeps a busy client blocked forever.",
        "Using t >= time - window, which keeps a request one second too long."
      ],
      "interviewExplanation": "Scanning the full log is correct but grows with total traffic, which a gateway can't afford.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "title": "Step 1",
          "content": "At times 1 and 2, ana has 0 and then 1 recent requests: accepted. At 3 she has 2: rejected."
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "title": "Step 2",
          "content": "At 11, only 2 > 1 counts (1 recent): accepted. At 12, only 11 counts: accepted."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "OPTIMAL",
      "language": "JAVASCRIPT",
      "title": "Per-client sliding log",
      "intuition": "Times only move forward, so a per-client queue can discard expired entries permanently.",
      "approach": "Map each client to a queue of accepted times (an array with a head index). Advance the head past times <= t - window. Reject if the remaining count >= limit; otherwise append t and accept.",
      "pseudocode": "logs = map client → queue\nfor (c, t): q = logs[c]; while q.front <= t - window: pop front\n  if |q| >= limit: out false else q.push(t); out true",
      "code": "function rateGate(limit, window, requests) {\n  const logs = new Map();\n  return requests.map(([client, time]) => {\n    if (!logs.has(client)) logs.set(client, { times: [], head: 0 });\n    const log = logs.get(client);\n    while (log.head < log.times.length && log.times[log.head] <= time - window) log.head++;\n    if (log.times.length - log.head >= limit) return false;\n    log.times.push(time);\n    return true;\n  });\n}",
      "timeComplexity": "O(1) amortized per request",
      "spaceComplexity": "O(clients × limit) live entries",
      "commonMistakes": [
        "Using shift() on large arrays, which costs O(n) per call; a head index keeps it O(1).",
        "Sharing one queue across clients."
      ],
      "interviewExplanation": "This is a sliding-log rate limiter. Each client has a queue of accepted timestamps, expired ones are popped from the front, and the request is allowed if fewer than limit remain. Amortized O(1), and memory is bounded by limit per active client if you compact the arrays. In production I'd compare it with a token bucket or a sliding-window counter, which use O(1) memory per client, and with a shared store like Redis when several gateway instances enforce the same limit.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "title": "Step 1",
          "content": "ana: [1], [1,2]; at 3 the queue holds 2 ≥ limit: reject."
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "title": "Step 2",
          "content": "At 11: drop 1 (1 <= 1), queue [2], accept → [2,11]. At 12: drop 2, accept."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
